            {
              "id": "414c5b16-4a38-31ce-938a-c46d13cfad0b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the `fwscanf` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled or if the variable `v` is not of the correct type or size.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that are not susceptible to format string vulnerabilities. Always validate and sanitize input data. Be especially careful with format strings, ensuring they are constant and not user-controlled.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fwscanf` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `fgets` to read the file into a buffer and then `sscanf` to parse the data, checking the return value to ensure the correct number of items were parsed:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n\nchar buffer[256];\nif (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), f) != NULL) {\n    if (sscanf(buffer, formatString, &v) != 1) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the compiler.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f76828c-4b06-3ae3-81d3-074ac0da35ac",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or information disclosure. One such function is `sscanf`.\n\nThe `sscanf` function reads data from a string and stores them according to the parameter format into the locations pointed by the additional arguments. The potential problem with `sscanf` is that it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can cause buffer overflow if the input is not properly validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `sscanf` and other unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking, such as `strncpy` for string copying, `snprintf` for string formatting, and `fgets` for reading lines of text.\n\nAlso, always validate and sanitize input data before processing it. Never trust user input blindly. Consider using libraries or frameworks that automatically handle these tasks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of `sscanf`, you can use `std::istringstream` from the C++ Standard Library for parsing strings. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string str = \"123 456\";\nint a, b;\n\nstd::istringstream iss(str);\niss >> a >> b;\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::istringstream` reads integers from the string `str` and stores them in `a` and `b`. If the string cannot be parsed as integers, `iss` will be in a fail state, and you can check this with `iss.fail()`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<sstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0d21f8f4-23a4-3a18-8833-bad675e8d04d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fwprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled or properly sanitized.\n\nFormat string vulnerabilities occur when the submitted data of an input string is evaluated as a command by the application. In this case, the application is vulnerable because it directly uses user input in the `fwprintf` function. This can allow an attacker to read the stack, write to arbitrary locations in memory, or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using functions that do not check the lengths of arguments. Instead, use safer versions of these functions that include length checks. \n\nIn the case of `fwprintf`, it is recommended to use a static format string or ensure that the format string is properly sanitized and does not contain any user-controllable input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nfwprintf(f, formatString, v);\n```\n\nUse a static format string:\n\n```cpp\nfwprintf(f, L\"%s\", formatString);\n```\n\nOr ensure that the format string is properly sanitized and does not contain any user-controllable input.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cwchar>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1439b63-2ff6-39a4-9f8f-ba32de35d36a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `std::localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded context. Instead, use their thread-safe counterparts. In this case, `std::localtime` should be replaced with `std::localtime_s` (on Windows) or `localtime_r` (on POSIX systems).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a sample fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\n// ...\n\ntime_t tt;\nstruct tm result;\n\n#ifdef _WIN32\nlocaltime_s(&result, &tt);\n#else\nlocaltime_r(&tt, &result);\n#endif\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<ctime>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "796646b8-21ee-305e-a114-8263e83efdbd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C library functions that are considered unsafe. These functions are unsafe because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation, which can lead to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows.\n\nIn this case, the function `atoi(s)` is used, which converts a string to an integer. This function does not check whether the conversion was successful or not, and if the string cannot be converted, it returns zero. This can lead to unexpected behavior if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform necessary validation. In the case of `atoi(s)`, a safer alternative is `strtol(s, &endptr, 10)`. This function returns a long integer, and if the conversion is unsuccessful, `endptr` will not point to the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(s)` function with `strtol(s, &endptr, 10)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    const char* s = \"123\";\n    char* endptr;\n    long int value = strtol(s, &endptr, 10);\n    if (*endptr != '\\0') {\n        std::cerr << \"Conversion error, non-convertible part: \" << endptr;\n    }\n    // use value...\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strtol(s, &endptr, 10)` is used to convert the string to a long integer. If the conversion is unsuccessful, `endptr` will not point to the null terminator, and an error message will be printed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header file contains declarations of general functions, including `strtol()`.\n- `<iostream>`: This header file defines the standard input/output stream objects.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "57075a9e-3ae1-3859-b515-f0b6b5c46751",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `strtok`.\n\nThe `strtok` function is used to split a string into tokens but it is not thread-safe. If it is used in multi-threaded applications, it can cause data races and lead to undefined behavior. It also modifies the input string, which can lead to unexpected behavior if the input string is not supposed to be modified.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `strtok` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `strtok_r` or `strtok_s` which are reentrant and safer to use in multi-threaded applications. Also, consider using C++ string handling functions or libraries that provide safer string manipulation functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strtok`, you can use `strtok_r` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar str[] = \"A string\\tof ,,tokens\\nand some  more tokens\";\nchar delims[] = \" \\t,.\\n\";\nchar *token = NULL;\nchar *saveptr = NULL;\n\nfor (token = strtok_r(str, delims, &saveptr); token != NULL; token = strtok_r(NULL, delims, &saveptr)) {\n    // process token\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstring` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
                  "57075a9e-3ae1-3859-b515-f0b6b5c46751"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a59be220-fee1-3b4b-a48e-9b972adf495d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `strtok`.\n\nThe `strtok` function is used to split a string into tokens but it is not thread-safe. If it is used in multi-threaded applications, it can cause data races and lead to undefined behavior. It also modifies the input string, which can lead to unexpected behavior if the input string is not supposed to be modified.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `strtok` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `strtok_r` or `strtok_s` which are reentrant and safer to use in multi-threaded applications. Also, consider using C++ string handling functions or libraries that provide safer string manipulation functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strtok`, you can use `strtok_r` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar str[] = \"A string\\tof ,,tokens\\nand some  more tokens\";\nchar delims[] = \" \\t,.\\n\";\nchar *token = NULL;\nchar *saveptr = NULL;\n\nfor (token = strtok_r(str, delims, &saveptr); token != NULL; token = strtok_r(NULL, delims, &saveptr)) {\n    // process token\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstring` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
