            {
              "id": "b304f3d6-9fcd-32f6-8b11-002abe10ac48",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nOne such function is `vsprintf`, which writes a formatted string to a buffer. The problem with `vsprintf` is that it does not check the size of the output buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the formatted string is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `vsprintf` and other similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking, such as `vsnprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `vsprintf` with `vsnprintf` and ensure that the size argument correctly reflects the size of the buffer. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdarg.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_printf(char *buffer, size_t size, const char *format, ...) {\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, size, format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `vsnprintf` will write at most `size - 1` characters to the buffer (leaving space for the null terminator), ensuring that the buffer is not overflowed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<stdarg.h>`: for handling variable argument lists\n- `<stdio.h>`: for `vsnprintf`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "87a38ae7-b5ca-35ba-a0d9-2cc4d3ddcc2c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. The `vfprintf` function is one such function. It writes output to the stream provided, under control of the string pointed to by format that specifies how subsequent arguments are converted for output.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `vfprintf` such as `vfprintf_s`, `vsnprintf`, or `vsnprintf_s` which are designed to prevent buffer overflows by requiring the size of the output buffer as an argument. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vfprintf` function with a safer alternative. For example, if you have the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n\nvoid myprint(FILE *fp, char *format, ...)\n{\n   va_list arg;\n   \n   va_start(arg, format);\n   vfprintf(fp, format, arg);\n   va_end(arg);\n}\n```\n\nYou can replace `vfprintf` with `vfprintf_s` like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n\nvoid myprint(FILE *fp, char *format, ...)\n{\n   va_list arg;\n   \n   va_start(arg, format);\n   vfprintf_s(fp, format, arg);\n   va_end(arg);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- stdio.h\n- stdarg.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d44a7f3e-0af5-3bab-b49d-34784ee2fb1e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to serious security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflow. In this case, the `vsprintf` function is used, which does not check the size of the output buffer, leading to potential buffer overflow if the input is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. For instance, instead of `vsprintf`, use `vsnprintf`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the size of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <cstdio>\n\n#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024\n\nvoid safe_function(char *buffer, const char *format, ...) {\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, BUFFER_SIZE, format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdarg>`: Provides macros to handle variable argument lists.\n- `<cstdio>`: Provides functions for input and output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
